# The arpeggiator from the panel (sim/web/README.md, "The arpeggiator"):
# ARP's tap switches it on and opens its pages; a chord arpeggiates; KNOB1
# steps the mode, KNOB2 the rate, KNOB4 the octaves; SELECT turns to RHYTHM
# for a pattern and back; ALGORITHM picks stock presets.
--button 0.05:ARP
--key 0.20:7:100:0.60
--key 0.20:11:90:0.60
--key 0.22:14:80:0.58
--turn 0.40:KNOB1:2
--turn 0.50:KNOB2:-2
--turn 0.55:KNOB4:1
--turn 0.60:SELECT:1
--turn 0.65:KNOB1:3
--turn 0.70:SELECT:-1
--turn 0.90:ALGORITHM:3
# PLAY/STOP: the sequencer's note joins on step 5; REC punches in and two
# keys are recorded as played; REC again ends the take, which comes back
# through the arp on the next pass.
--button 1.00:PLAY/STOP
--button 1.20:REC
--key 1.40:9:100:0.40
--key 1.42:12:100:0.38
--button 2.10:REC
# ARP held: Latch (the notes play on after the keys go up); another chord
# replaces the latched one; PLAY/STOP stops (the track's notes leave the
# arp, the latched chord played by hand stays); the arp keeps playing the
# latched chord from the sequencer's tempo.
--button 2.30:ARP:0.70
--key 2.85:5:100:0.10
--key 2.87:9:100:0.10
--key 3.30:2:100:0.10
--key 3.31:7:100:0.10
--button 3.70:PLAY/STOP
# ARP's tap switches it off (its notes end at once) and closes the pages;
# a key plays plainly.
--button 4.20:ARP
--key 4.30:7:100:0.20
